  "context": "## Tacit Knowledge Management in Distributed Tiger Teams\nTacit knowledge is treated as an operational capability, not merely undocumented information. Tiger Teams surface, test, transmit and preserve the judgment embedded in people, routines and places.\n\nIn mission-critical transformation, not all relevant knowledge appears in procedures, databases, KPIs, dashboards or formal reports.\n\nIt lives in experienced operators, informal routines, workshop habits, field judgement, apprenticeship relationships, local rituals, shared timing, physical spaces, trusted conversations and subtle ways of coordinating that an external observer may not immediately understand.\n\nThey redesign processes, replace systems, change reporting structures, introduce dashboards, move teams, alter spaces, automate tasks or reorganise responsibilities without understanding how tacit knowledge was being transmitted.\n\nWhen that happens, the organisation may appear more modern, but it can lose capabilities it does not know how to reconstruct.\nEtiquetas: Capacidad humana y deuda de inteligencia 25.64%; Casos de uso y aplicaciones 5.13%; Decisiones y responsabilidad 10.26%; Economía, costes y liquidez 5.13%; Empresa y gestión 12.82%; Evidencia, procedencia y atribución 5.13%; Memoria, comunidad y territorio 10.25%; Transformación y continuidad operativa 25.64%\nFicha: https://dakleyer.github.io/perspective-beyond/sources/tacit-knowledge-management-in-distributed-tiger-teams-n1287/\nDocumento de origen: https://jubap.net/tacit-knowledge-management-in-distributed-tiger-teams/\n\n",
